# @PROJECT_DISPLAY_NAME@

C++@PROJECT_CPP_VERSION@ executable with the CMake target `@PROJECT_NAME@`.

## Development

Install CMake @PROJECT_CMAKE_VERSION@+, Ninja, Git, and a compiler supporting C++@PROJECT_CPP_VERSION@. On macOS,
install the Xcode command line tools. On Windows, install Visual Studio C++
build tools and run commands from a Developer Command Prompt.

```sh
./setup.sh
./run.sh
```

On Windows use `setup.bat` and `run.bat`. Pass `release` to either launcher
for a release build; debug is the default. Manual builds:

@PROJECT_BUILD_GUIDE@

`scaffold` is destructive, one-time project initialization. `setup` is repeatable
machine setup. `run` configures, builds, and runs the CMake `run` target.
Another developer cloning this generated project only needs setup and run.
Setup without `--clean` reuses dependencies and build output. `--clean` discards
all setup output and rebuilds dependencies from fresh downloads.

## Dependencies

Edit `vcpkg.json` to add dependencies, then rerun setup. The starter dependency
is `fmt`. Setup clones the latest vcpkg into ignored `external/vcpkg/`, preserves
its `.git`, bootstraps it, and installs the manifest into `vcpkg_installed/`.
Later setup runs reuse that copy. There is no pinned revision or submodule.
For a fresh clone and setup, run `./setup.sh --clean` (Windows: `setup.bat --clean`).
Clean setup bypasses binary and asset caches and downloads dependencies again.
**Warning:** this deletes `external/vcpkg/`, `vcpkg_installed/`, and `build/`,
including local changes. Source files and the project's `.git` remain intact.
Use `./setup.sh release --clean` for a clean release setup.

Dependency installation belongs to setup; run does not install dependencies.
Development configuration disables vcpkg's automatic manifest installation.
vcpkg chooses its native triplet. To override it, set `VCPKG_DEFAULT_TRIPLET`
in your environment before setup and keep it set during configuration and run.
Custom configurations must use the same triplet and `VCPKG_INSTALLED_DIR` as setup.

@PROJECT_GIT_GUIDE@

## Scaffold source

When generated at the template root, `.scaffold/` and `scaffold-here.sh` /
`scaffold-here.bat` stay available for reuse. External destinations contain only
the generated project. Re-scaffolding requires confirmation and replaces generated
source changes. Build and development scripts work independently of `.scaffold/`.

## Sources and assets

Sources are discovered recursively in `src/`. @PROJECT_SOURCE_DISCOVERY_GUIDE@
Include `src/util/strings.hpp`
as `"util/strings.hpp"`; only `src/` is the private include root.
Link additional CMake dependency targets in `CMakeLists.txt`.

Files in `assets/` are copied beside the executable after linking, for any
CMake generator. The run target uses that directory as its working directory.
After changing assets without changing code, relink or clean and rebuild to
refresh the post-build copy.
